; expect 42
; A char literal is a CHARACTER, and a character is not a byte.
;
; stage1 hardcoded a char literal as three bytes - quote, one byte,
; quote - and decoded it with a single `strByte`. So `'é'` failed to
; parse at all (its literal is four bytes), and any that did parse
; answered the lead byte of the encoding rather than the code point.
; stage0 lexes a Vec<char> and has always been right, which made this
; a silent divergence between the two compilers on ordinary source.
;
; All four sequence widths, checked against the values stage0 gives:
; 'A' 65, 'é' 233, '世' 19990, '😀' 128512. The answer is 42 rather
; than a code point because an exit status is one byte and 19990
; would arrive as 22.
(:: main Int)
(fn (main)
  (if (&& (== (cast Int 'A') 65)
          (&& (== (cast Int 'é') 233)
              (&& (== (cast Int '世') 19990)
                  (== (cast Int '😀') 128512))))
      42
      7))
